{"name":"Surfie Green","hex":"#007b77","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"surfie","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A surfer (one who rides a surfboard), especially one involved in the surfing subculture."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/surfie"},{"term":"green","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The color of grass and leaves; a primary additive color midway between yellow and blue which is evoked by light between roughly 495–570 nm.","A member of a green party; an environmentalist."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a green hue.","Sickly, unwell."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/green"}],"entries":[{"title":"Surf culture","blocks":[{"text":"Surf culture includes the people, language, fashion, and lifestyle surrounding the sport of surfing. The history of surfing began with the ancient Polynesians. That initial culture directly influenced modern surfing, which began to flourish and evolve in the early 20th century, with its popularity peaking during the 1950s and 1960s. It has affected music, fashion, literature, film, art, and youth jargon in popular culture. The number of surfers throughout the world continues to increase as the culture spreads."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d1/2010_mavericks_competition_edit1.jpg/330px-2010_mavericks_competition_edit1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Surf_culture"},{"title":"Green","blocks":[{"text":"Green is the color between cyan and yellow on the visible spectrum. It has 295–350 different shades. It is evoked by light which has a dominant wavelength of roughly 495–570 nm. In subtractive color systems, used in painting and color printing, it is created by a combination of yellow and cyan; in the RGB color model, used on television and computer screens, it is one of the additive primary colors, along with red and blue, which are mixed in different combinations to create all other colors. By far the largest contributor to green in nature is chlorophyll, the chemical by which plants photosynthesize and convert sunlight into chemical energy. Many creatures have adapted to their green environments by taking on a green hue themselves as camouflage. Several minerals have a green color, including the emerald, which is colored green by its chromium content."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/23/Champ_de_bl%C3%A9_C%C3%B4te-d%27Or_Bourgogne_avril_2014.jpg/330px-Champ_de_bl%C3%A9_C%C3%B4te-d%27Or_Bourgogne_avril_2014.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Green"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4a/Pontiac_Woodie.jpg/330px-Pontiac_Woodie.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4a/Pontiac_Woodie.jpg/330px-Pontiac_Woodie.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Pontiac woodie, used by early surfers","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APontiac_Woodie.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/29/Alphonse_Pellion%2C_%C3%8Eles_Sandwich%3B_Maisons_de_Kra%C3%AFmokou%2C_Premier_Ministre_du_Roi%3B_Fabrication_des_%C3%89toffes_%28c._1819%29.jpg/500px-Alphonse_Pellion%2C_%C3%8Eles_Sandwich%3B_Maisons_de_Kra%C3%AFmokou%2C_Premier_Ministre_du_Roi%3B_Fabrication_des_%C3%89toffes_%28c._1819%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/29/Alphonse_Pellion%2C_%C3%8Eles_Sandwich%3B_Maisons_de_Kra%C3%AFmokou%2C_Premier_Ministre_du_Roi%3B_Fabrication_des_%C3%89toffes_%28c._1819%29.jpg/500px-Alphonse_Pellion%2C_%C3%8Eles_Sandwich%3B_Maisons_de_Kra%C3%AFmokou%2C_Premier_Ministre_du_Roi%3B_Fabrication_des_%C3%89tof